From 53884bbfb9df27899ab89d54ab62cc2191907aa5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 16:22:35 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(entities): evaluate named actor properties and
 pre-animation

Retain immutable named actor property snapshots across sparse updates and
bind query.property and query.has_property before pre_animation and render
controller selection. Reevaluate custom actor selections on ticks so a
property or time-driven controller can change the visible model.

Correct Mocha parser and evaluator boundaries for string equality,
assignment/ternary precedence and unary calls. CubeCraft controllers use
all three behaviors, and Geyser mushroom permutations negate property
queries. Shared semantic tests cover enum comparison, assignment targets,
conditional branches and negated calls.

The Bedrock Molang reference documents string comparison and operator
precedence: https://mojang.github.io/bedrock-samples/Molang.html . Typed
wire-property decoding remains in the separate metadata feature.

Evaluate render-controller material arrays and bone overrides alongside actor inputs.
Retain ignore_lighting and the evaluated light_color_multiplier in immutable
selected models so a client renderer can preserve native draw state. These
properties are present in the saved protocol2193 Hive pack, including its
logo controller, and in the target Bedrock1.26.51.1 render-controller format.

Preserve the retained native actor scale from FLOAT metadata index 38 and
bind query.model_scale before controller evaluation. The protocol 2193
CubeCraft scene sends 1.7 for eleven banners and 5 for its hanging cube;
the target Bedrock 1.26.51.1 reference renders those larger meshes. Native
query.model_scale is registered at 1421f22c8 and its getter at 14221ac00
reads the float context member at +0x110. The documented query name is
model_scale, not scale: https://mojang.github.io/bedrock-samples/Molang.html .

Refresh custom models on scale-only changes so sparse metadata updates
reach both native client integrations and converted item displays. Multiply
the converter fit and origin translation by the actor scale, retaining
zero and negative values without introducing a Java living-entity clamp.
Client scripts.scale and camera-driven animations remain separate work.

Semantic tests decode the FLOAT wire value, retain it across unrelated
metadata updates, exercise equal-model refreshes, and select controllers
using query.model_scale.

Carry immutable controller UV offset/scale expression metadata in evaluated
models and include it in equality-based model updates. Protocol workers
preserve camera-dependent expressions for the native client's draw
context instead of evaluating them against unavailable camera state.
Native Bedrock 1.26.51.1 (protocol 2193) controller parser 141cabf60
initializes offset to zero and scale to one; ENTITY vertex DXIL applies
normalized UV times scale plus offset. The licensed executable SHA-256 is
537c0aee2e79afbdc94b44b28e00f466ae62bc50e2733d953b430db9dbaa9ee7.
Model-selection tests evaluate retained lifetime expressions after a
snapshot replacement and check that UV-only changes refresh the model.
Native exports and saved server assets remain private.

Use the pinned MochaFloats 6.1.0 interpreter for single-precision values
and every arithmetic operation. Mocha 3.0.1 evaluates in doubles, so
casting only the returned value cannot reproduce native rounding in
controller conditions or assignments. Raise the core toolchain to Java
25 and migrate bindings to the fork's org.redlance namespace. Depend on
its interpreter modules without the unused bytecode compiler. Retain our
string comparison, assignment precedence and unary/postfix corrections.

The official Molang property guide documents float evaluation and the
16.7-million integer precision boundary. Shared regressions exercise
intermediate arithmetic, equality, assignments and property inputs:
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/minecraft/creator/documents/introductiontoentityproperties

Resolve runtime, parser and lexer 6.1.0 from Maven Central. Remove the
private Maven repository so the shared runtime uses the published release:
https://repo.maven.apache.org/maven2/org/redlance/mochafloats/runtime/6.1.0/runtime-6.1.0.pom

Retain named unused parameters in Checkstyle because independently
replayed upstream patches still compile on Java 17. Keep Java 25 syntax
and semantic checks active; the toolchain upgrade does not require a
mechanical rename across those unrelated patches.

Share native Molang easing and trigonometry in core with client animation.
Preserve directed rotation interpolation and the target's [-180,180)
angle reduction, including the float addition before the remainder.
The previous library sorted endpoints and repeatedly subtracted turns,
which reverses animations and can stall on large finite float values.

Target Bedrock 1.26.51.1/protocol 2193 constant dispatcher 140ae6140 and
rotation helper 140aeb870 match runtime operations 140b2c2b0 and
140b2d270 across 9,367 angle and interpolation cases. The private emulator
supplies numeric nodes and stacks, mocks eligibility and cleanup, and
supplies the fmodf import with host C float remainder. Native arithmetic
and runtime stack operations execute unchanged. This establishes these
math operations, not the complete parser or visible animation.
The official Molang reference describes directed shortest interpolation:
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/minecraft/creator/reference/content/molangreference/examples/molangconcepts/mathfunctions/math_lerprotate

Publish the complete evaluated controller snapshot under the actual Java
actor UUID when a client negotiates viabedrock:custom_entity. Both direct
clients and ViaProxy use this ordered state channel. Preserve model and
material order, UV expressions, lighting, empty selections and signed scale.
Remove snapshots with their actor; late capability registration replaces
existing display parts and republishes live actors. Clients without the
capability retain the existing converted Java item-display representation.

Bound payload bytes, string lengths and model/material counts before
allocating untrusted state. Reject duplicate bindings and mismatched wire
versions instead of installing partial state. Six semantic codec tests
cover round trips, immutable ownership, clearing versus removal, signed
zero, invalid headers/counts, truncation, duplicate bindings and byte limits.

This transports the evaluated state already used by the direct renderer.
Native actor resources use the accepted converted resource-pack archive;
no local Bedrock installation or frontend Store account is required.
Player properties, actor events and animation timing remain separate work.

Publish immutable actor inputs through viabedrock:actor_state_v2 after the
Java play join and client capability registration. Retain native runtime
identity, flags, typed named properties, variants, scale, raw name and
optional raw spell color. Bind snapshots and removals to a stable per-actor lifetime
UUID so replacing a Java or runtime identity cannot retain stale state.
Local-player messages explicitly identify the frontend avatar mapping.

Publish after registration and accepted sparse state changes, deduplicate
unchanged snapshots, and replay retained live state for late negotiation.
The shared client-thread registry resolves native runtime IDs and guards
removal against the current incarnation. Ordinary Java translation does
not depend on this channel. Eight semantic tests cover complete codecs,
immutability, malformed inputs and memory bounds, sparse native snapshots,
local identity resolution, stale removal, ID reuse and independent stores.

The named property and flag values come from the target protocol2193 core
state already used by direct rendering. Their Molang consumers remain
client rendering concerns. The general query/actor lifetime semantics are
documented at https://mojang.github.io/bedrock-samples/Molang.html .
Events, interpolation and complete animation behavior remain separate.

Preserve NAME metadata formatting and case for native global transforms.
Use wire version two and a new negotiated channel so older frontends do
not read the changed snapshot layout. Native player gamertag initialization
and death-counter lifecycle remain separate verification work.

Retain native player blocking timestamps in the client-thread actor
registry. Sample flags once per active simulation tick, preserve rising
edges outside the timer guards, and reset timers on actor replacement,
runtime identity reuse, removal, and connection cleanup. The same core
state model serves direct and ViaProxy client integrations without
changing the actor snapshot transport.

Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372 starts the local player's blocking clock
on a TRANSITION_BLOCKING rising edge while BLOCKING is set. Its shield
bob callback checks that start clock but subtracts the damaged-shield
clock. Preserve unsigned elapsed conversion and the native float clamp.
A licensed native watchpoint confirms the start write and reset. Private
instruction fixtures cover 120 start branches, 1728 block/damage branches,
and 2700 bob callback cases. Keep proprietary inputs outside the patch.

Supply the native equip-height and retained-item update in core. Advance
independent hand heights by at most 0.4 per simulation tick, preserve the
previous interpolation value, and copy on immediate replacement or at
height 0.1 inclusively. Target Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372 has no Java
hands-busy or attack-cooldown branch in this update.

Match 2304 actual native instruction cases plus 24 exact copy boundaries.
Observe and skip native stack-copy calls rather than emulate their internals.
Item classification and complete visible swap parity remain separate work.

Share the token-based Molang parser with client consumers instead of
maintaining a separate client grammar and core text rewriting. Match
statement admission, decimal fractions, assignment precedence and
complex-expression zero results against the matching 1.26.51.1 binary.
Keep query bindings and execution limits with their consumers. Native
constant evaluation covers 21 declarations and 105 billboard samples;
variable registration and pack-version gates remain unverified.

Compile return, break, continue, loop and for_each into private core host
functions so nested frames unwind before later mutations. Keep Mocha's
float arithmetic and property bindings. The target Bedrock 1.26.51.1 VM
returns through selected nested scopes and decrements positive float
loop remainders once per iteration. Native count 0.5 runs once, 1.5 twice,
and 1025 runs 1025 times. Empty scopes and malformed loop bodies reject.
The compiler and VM execute unchanged in private probes of 57 declarations
and 285 billboard samples, including eight temporary-variable cases.
Native registry constructors run unchanged for the variable cases; complete
actor and world contexts remain absent. A shared 16384-iteration host
budget retains the client's existing bound across nested and successive
loops. It is our untrusted-work policy, not a proven native engine limit.
Tests preserve nearest-loop jumps, returns, mutation order, array bindings,
syntax recovery and the shared budget. Native array context remains unverified.

Offer caller-owned evaluation groups in core so related expressions can
share a temporary binding without mutating the caller's scope. Single
expression calls retain fresh temporary storage. Persistent actor bindings
remain shared. Each expression has its own control-flow frames, current
value and host work budget, including nested evaluations.

Target Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372 billboard updater 1462b1d70 reads
size, ordinary UV coordinates and extents, then custom direction axes in
one native temporary store. Nine declarations and 45 native samples
preserve writes across those expression calls. Native 1421fcb60 clears
that store before each isolated sample. The registry, parser, compiler,
VM, component updater and store cleanup execute unchanged; host allocation,
CRT operations and locks are supplied. Full particle/frame reset boundaries
and undefined temporary reads remain separate verification work.

Tests cover temporary aliasing, numeric and string values, early returns,
changing current values, persistent bindings and independent groups.

Distinguish missing variable members from valid zero and empty strings.
Native 1.26.51.1 VM faults abort the expression before later statements,
retain completed writes, and let null coalescing catch an invalid left
operand without catching return, break or continue. Lower variable member
reads and coalescing into private runtime functions while preserving
assignment and array-loop targets. Query access remains with its consumer.

Private native probes cover 67 declarations and 335 component samples,
including a supplied empty native actor-variable map, temporary members,
strings, short-circuit branches, loops, arithmetic and nested jump handling.
The matching registry, compiler, VM and billboard functions execute
unchanged. Allocation, CRT operations, TLS, locks and diagnostics are host
boundaries; complete actor/world context remains unverified.

Reject unparenthesized assignment chains before evaluation, as the target
compiler does. Parenthesized assignments remain admitted. Native embedded
assignment results depend on accumulator state and compiler optimizations;
that broader behavior remains separate work. This change does not claim
assignment-result parity, complete frame scope or all runtime diagnostics.
